eful base64 encoding of a short string.\n\nLet me choose SVG string:\n`<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\"><rect width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4f7cff\"/><circle cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\"/></svg>`\n\nLength ~150 chars. Base64 requires processing 3 bytes at a time. That's 50 groups — doable but error prone. I'll attempt carefully, but a mistake is likely.\n\nAlternative: I could do base64 of a tiny string and pad rest... no.\n\nHonestly, I can try to compute. Let me instead choose a well-known base64 snippet I'm confident about. A common one: a 1x1 png base64:\n`iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==` — that's a known 1x1 transparent/blue PNG. This is a widely-cited base64 for 1x1 pixel PNG (blue). Yes: `data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==` is a known 1x1 blue-ish pixel PNG. I recall this exact string from pixel.png references. It's commonly cited as a 1x1 transparent PNG? Actually the famous transparent one is `iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAQAAAC1HAwCAAAAC0lEQVR42mNkYPhfDwAChwGA60e6kgAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==`. The one with `42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQ` decodes to a 1x1 with color. It's used in many tests. I'll trust it as valid PNG.\n\nBut a 1x1 pixel as \"image resource\" is lame visually. I can use it in an <img> with CSS background behind, or use as a subtle logo alongside styled div. Better: use multiple known-good base64 images? I only confidently know the 1x1.\n\nAlternatively, encode SVG base64 for simple ones and verify carefully. Let me try to encode a small SVG properly.\n\nActually, I can reasonably encode base64 if I do it systematically. Let me encode this SVG:\n\n`<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"45\" fill=\"#2f80ed\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">¥</text></svg>`\n\nThat's ~200 bytes → ~270 base64 chars. Hand-encoding 67 triplets is very error-prone.\n\nPragmatic approach: I'll hand-encode a moderate SVG and accept small risk? A broken image in screenshot test is bad. The known 1x1 PNG string is safe. I can use the 1x1 PNG as an <img> inside a styled logo wrapper with a background gradient div — the img satisfies the requirement even if it's a pixel. To make it more visible, I can stretch it: <img> with width/height and background... a solid blue pixel stretched = solid blue square. Actually that works as a colored dot logo! Style it with border-radius. It's deterministic and valid.\n\nAlso I can use CSS background-image with the same data URI for a texture — same pixel stretched.\n\nI'll use the known PNG: `data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==` — I believe this is a valid 1x1 PNG (blue). Let me double check memory: This string appears widely as \"1x1 blue pixel png base64\". Yes, commonly `iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==`. Good.\n\nAdditionally, attempt one hand-encoded SVG for a nicer hero illustration? Risky. Instead, build hero phone mockup with pure CSS. Use the pixel PNG images as avatar dots etc.\n\nActually, let me try encoding one more known base64: the famous red dot 1x1: `iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAAC0lEQVR42mP8/x8AAwMCAO+ip1sAAAAASUVORK5CYII=`? Not sure. Stick with the one I know; use CSS filters? No, keep simple. One image usage is enough (\"至少一处\").\n\nI'll use it twice (hero logo dot and feature avatar) — same data URI.\n\nNow design: Chinese personal finance app \"简账\" landing page.\n\nSections:\n1. data-oey-section=\"hero\" — nav + hero: badge, h1, p, buttons, phone mockup (CSS), logo img.\n2. data-oey-section=\"features\" — heading + 3 cards (each with icon via CSS/emoji? emoji is fine, deterministic). Each